  'Theory of Architecture is an advanced seminar in architectural discourse, criticism, and design thought. Students read foundational and contemporary texts alongside buildings, cities, drawings, exhibitions, and media. The course develops the ability to interpret architecture as cultural argument, political practice, sensory experience, technical imagination, historical construction, and public responsibility.',
  $tharjson${"slug": "theory-of-architecture", "title": "Theory of Architecture", "description": "Theory of Architecture is an advanced seminar in architectural discourse, criticism, and design thought. Students read foundational and contemporary texts alongside buildings, cities, drawings, exhibitions, and media. The course develops the ability to interpret architecture as cultural argument, political practice, sensory experience, technical imagination, historical construction, and public responsibility.", "topics": [{"id": "88710dd6-feae-402d-8c55-fd06b300e28d", "type": "topic", "title": "What Is Architectural Theory?", "description": "This opening section frames theory as a disciplined way of asking what architecture is, what it does, who it serves, and how it can be judged. Students learn that theory is not an ornament added to design; it is a set of tools for seeing assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "9fd2253b-fd9a-4a68-9975-b2b2a4c3a7aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory as Question and Method", "children": [{"id": "bd0e020e-a025-4b4d-afdc-8790ab4a45e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts, claims, evidence, and critique"}, {"id": "a0ffbf9c-2dd8-4e12-a1ea-dd37fa73c862", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory versus history, criticism, and design method"}, {"id": "12409c26-7529-40f5-9d56-c9c7a5063c15", "type": "concept", "title": "Normative, descriptive, speculative, and political theory"}, {"id": "e67d1255-ec23-43f1-b7fc-8d395b94401b", "type": "concept", "title": "How theory changes what designers notice"}], "description": "Students define theory as a way of formulating questions, concepts, and arguments about buildings and cities."}, {"id": "3da0181e-4de3-4df9-a8d2-9136d166c47a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as Discipline and Practice", "children": [{"id": "58ed0743-f77c-422f-be58-eccffef6f259", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture, building, urbanism, and landscape"}, {"id": "32a8dad7-b6cc-45ae-9c95-39f348824714", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, expertise, and institutional authority"}, {"id": "4f8ec3c1-49de-43d7-94c6-1d81395c3487", "type": "concept", "title": "Studio culture and disciplinary reproduction"}, {"id": "d526575d-1678-49ab-b870-6c282f293bd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Practice, pedagogy, publication, and public debate"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how architecture became a profession, a discipline, and a cultural field."}, {"id": "e49e429a-86f9-4ed6-852d-15748e15387b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading Buildings and Texts Together", "children": [{"id": "c2273ea5-b076-443c-a9bf-17c84624f493", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading of texts"}, {"id": "baece6f6-37de-41e9-b1f1-cf46276853d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal, spatial, material, and urban analysis"}, {"id": "c715b985-335b-42e0-9b16-d8196f699bc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifestoes, treatises, criticism, and interviews"}, {"id": "f3381c17-3f39-4a7e-b453-0870276c81f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Using theory to frame a design position"}], "description": "Students learn to move between written theory and spatial evidence without reducing one to the other."}]}, {"id": "6a67a339-8eb4-4cfa-8df1-dd926d52799f", "type": "topic", "title": "Vitruvius, Classical Theory, and the Origins of Architectural Discourse", "description": "This section introduces classical architectural theory as a durable source of vocabulary, hierarchy, proportion, and professional identity. The point is not to revere the canon, but to understand how architectural authority was constructed.", "children": [{"id": "a495b883-851d-4179-b73a-979751177347", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vitruvian Categories and Professional Identity", "children": [{"id": "7fcffe97-7c43-4ac8-bc49-1082e040022d", "type": "concept", "title": "Firmitas, utilitas, venustas"}, {"id": "57dc28a0-3f56-4447-af93-0af1a01c44cb", "type": "concept", "title": "The architect's education and breadth of knowledge"}, {"id": "7620ac19-ec49-4aeb-af27-cac6b34faf73", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity, firmness, delight, and later translations"}, {"id": "d39d4689-3352-43fa-97ab-0cde7da8320d", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, public works, and civic responsibility"}], "description": "Students examine how Vitruvius linked architecture to knowledge, ethics, public life, and the body."}, {"id": "3b5bcbb0-186f-4e09-93c3-75dcb49622b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Orders, Proportion, and the Body", "children": [{"id": "53b2d7c0-8c0f-4cc5-8eb3-4b17ae654ac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Column orders and decorum"}, {"id": "b5598f88-615b-43f2-babd-e08a319e988c", "type": "concept", "title": "Anthropometry, symmetry, and harmony"}, {"id": "6931a211-d887-42f1-ae83-4613532de9ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Temple, house, theater, and city as theoretical objects"}, {"id": "13689302-53b1-4d5d-9da9-692c4798e4f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered and political assumptions in classical proportion"}], "description": "Classical theory makes proportion and the body central to architectural judgment."}, {"id": "28e67f9f-88e5-461a-b817-35c166a942b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical Theory's Afterlives", "children": [{"id": "a020c26e-4350-431f-b5f0-209e035b9c5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation and pedagogy"}, {"id": "0e248f20-aba5-4273-9c79-92c6cd9b54c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Classicism as universal language and political symbol"}, {"id": "aec805c5-72df-4c6c-b145-4171709bc01a", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial and state uses of classical imagery"}, {"id": "ffeccf3a-414e-427f-8663-63b7f62524ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique, parody, and reinterpretation"}], "description": "Students track how classical ideas return in Renaissance, Enlightenment, Beaux-Arts, modern, and postmodern discourse."}]}, {"id": "dfea18ea-b467-4c84-a275-ec201c90e2f7", "type": "topic", "title": "Renaissance Humanism: Alberti, Palladio, Proportion, and Treatises", "description": "This unit studies the Renaissance as a moment when architectural theory became textual, authorial, humanist, and print-based. Students learn how theory shaped design practice, professional identity, and historical revival.", "children": [{"id": "6a2830e5-7bdd-4ac4-8096-13916c5b223b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alberti and Architecture as Liberal Art", "children": [{"id": "277bfaac-d867-4c30-80cc-4fcada5ada19", "type": "concept", "title": "Lineamenta and design as mental work"}, {"id": "f56cf93b-5b51-462a-a696-572af055ccb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Beauty, concinnitas, and proportion"}, {"id": "25cb268b-c35c-4209-a1b2-7668b38a06ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, city, and civic order"}, {"id": "4fe33f31-f0bb-4731-bd85-4648927b895a", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as professional elevation"}], "description": "Students study Alberti's claim that architecture belongs to intellectual culture, not only building craft."}, {"id": "a5435129-7a9a-46e5-8b1b-6688a43b2bf7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Palladio, Typology, and the Treatise", "children": [{"id": "c6723d12-a4e9-49e0-a9f6-b128f84606d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Villa, palace, church, and bridge types"}, {"id": "9a15617e-757b-4d9c-84ac-caebf3d6a108", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan, elevation, section, and measured precedent"}, {"id": "33acfa74-32f4-4550-8b34-67d9e573dc91", "type": "concept", "title": "Publication and dissemination of authority"}, {"id": "668753bd-3184-4da5-9d9d-4e9d85684f47", "type": "concept", "title": "Palladianism as transnational theory"}], "description": "Palladio shows how drawings, measurements, precedents, and books circulate architectural knowledge."}, {"id": "b32d2965-1963-4146-9d59-b3ab6f2c0ed4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanism, Perspective, and Representation", "children": [{"id": "ebf38862-2cbc-491a-99e3-6498c80da0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Perspective and visual order"}, {"id": "2f573f96-4c85-4c3a-a1ce-f561389fca37", "type": "concept", "title": "Architectural drawing as theoretical evidence"}, {"id": "32a36334-d3e3-4aaf-bcae-f5dbd8187930", "type": "concept", "title": "Human measure and ideal bodies"}, {"id": "f4980c0d-2ae7-46b7-81fd-021d16b9b29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of humanist universality"}], "description": "This subtopic links theory to drawing, vision, authorship, and the human subject."}]}, {"id": "bae05267-1a34-4fbc-9dce-11278452e809", "type": "topic", "title": "Enlightenment Theory: Taste, Sublime, Reason, Type, and Publics", "description": "This section explores theory as public argument about taste, reason, sensation, morality, institutions, and historical knowledge. Students see architecture as part of Enlightenment debates about society.", "children": [{"id": "4d33a72a-6a0b-4307-ad2e-5027f3dc2e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taste, Judgment, and the Public Sphere", "children": [{"id": "22add8a6-dc48-41e6-9ee7-a5df8f64d64b", "type": "concept", "title": "Taste, beauty, judgment, and education"}, {"id": "4447dd5f-d21c-4452-a3fc-c705b6033c8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism, academies, salons, and publics"}, {"id": "191d0592-c3c3-44db-a0f7-efc737c9f28b", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture as moral improvement"}, {"id": "52fd6eb9-8a3c-43f3-9634-7d5bd3bcb0d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal judgment versus cultural difference"}], "description": "Students examine how architecture became a matter of public criticism and cultivated judgment."}, {"id": "c700ac8d-1d7f-4499-af17-77ce2cc73d60", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Sublime, Character, and Sensation", "children": [{"id": "0585f44b-dadc-44c5-96a1-88e40c76e8e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Burke, Kant, and aesthetic categories"}, {"id": "0dd56542-fb4b-4bc7-b7da-dc22cac39fc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Character, expression, and emotional effect"}, {"id": "aebcf430-fdea-47b0-b928-404c3124fab8", "type": "concept", "title": "Picturesque, ruin, and landscape"}, {"id": "2a38cc1f-e088-4e0c-a2d9-6e0c6fd51e59", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture as atmosphere and event"}], "description": "The sublime brought fear, scale, darkness, and affect into architectural discourse."}, {"id": "dae7e703-686b-419b-8365-e5ec77605807", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Type, History, and Rational Ordering", "children": [{"id": "6e17bc7a-f44e-470b-b721-62d4b845ae26", "type": "concept", "title": "Quatremere de Quincy and type"}, {"id": "e2d8c7ff-b3c4-4396-b0e8-006ec54d818e", "type": "concept", "title": "Ledoux, Boullee, and visionary institutions"}, {"id": "1d60cb8e-fb3d-452b-841c-cdfecb0542de", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeology, historicism, and progress"}, {"id": "6614c26c-2f9a-4fec-b599-903a63af58de", "type": "concept", "title": "Prisons, museums, hospitals, schools, and civic reform"}], "description": "Students connect Enlightenment reason to classification, typology, and institutional design."}]}, {"id": "48c1c8c9-d5b2-49de-aaab-b11356bd6de0", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Industrialization, and the Birth of the Manifesto", "description": "This unit links architectural theory to the social and technological shocks of industrial modernity. Students learn why the manifesto became a privileged modern form of architectural argument.", "children": [{"id": "8aa3ba66-5c7c-4c21-8eac-ffc0bdaa0fc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industry, Technology, and New Building Types", "children": [{"id": "7d801d7d-f8bb-4e82-a892-8c7d8e6be6e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Iron, glass, steel, reinforced concrete, and elevators"}, {"id": "d5de2ea3-f5c5-431e-9619-7a7702169108", "type": "concept", "title": "Engineering and architecture as rival authorities"}, {"id": "0a2a36e3-aa42-4b19-b6a9-5d38609b1e62", "type": "concept", "title": "Expositions and display culture"}, {"id": "84e63a28-3fa3-47dc-8aec-5bd3ebd8fc40", "type": "concept", "title": "The city as machine and network"}], "description": "Modern theory emerges from railways, factories, bridges, exhibitions, office towers, and infrastructure."}, {"id": "95837a92-1e6f-4ea9-90a9-37b7160ee4ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Manifestoes and Avant-Garde Rhetoric", "children": [{"id": "40fb3b77-d562-4df1-9d53-b8fb4edda070", "type": "concept", "title": "Futurist, Constructivist, De Stijl, and Bauhaus texts"}, {"id": "ae59ca4b-5e07-477f-8eed-503191cdebf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Polemic, brevity, image, and shock"}, {"id": "3a6e8ed6-fe49-4e40-9102-70f94d12e05f", "type": "concept", "title": "New man, new life, and social transformation"}, {"id": "a905f12d-cf8d-4daf-ba50-4f5f14f64876", "type": "concept", "title": "The risk of totalizing claims"}], "description": "Students learn how manifestoes declare enemies, futures, audiences, and design programs."}, {"id": "8af4176d-31c3-4e7a-b846-e18fc33544df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and the Modern Architectural Public", "children": [{"id": "a87cb670-7661-4ef8-8977-ab666a48fcd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and canon formation"}, {"id": "fd8c941a-7595-499d-b2d9-2be9e3d5fc8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Architectural journals and international networks"}, {"id": "5d3acfc3-c48b-4277-be82-3802239defe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions as theory-making machines"}, {"id": "145d9e24-c8ef-43db-998a-ac6f3fcb5766", "type": "concept", "title": "Design as public persuasion"}], "description": "Journals, photographs, exhibitions, and books made modern architecture a discursive movement."}]}, {"id": "d7e2ee16-08da-4418-a547-fb50e8e7c407", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernist Theory: Function, Form, Social Reform, and the Avant-Garde", "description": "This section studies modernist theory as multiple, conflicted, and political. Students compare functionalism, social reform, abstraction, standardization, and technological optimism.", "children": [{"id": "f96311b5-dc67-487c-9802-22b1a67d114e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functionalism and the New Objectivity", "children": [{"id": "1cce34f6-6c3b-4860-b5e9-203ece691d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Form follows function and its interpretations"}, {"id": "69f50cf4-8eec-4260-a990-84eaef956465", "type": "concept", "title": "Neue Sachlichkeit and rational design"}, {"id": "d695cf0c-1c24-427b-8fca-4d8b00ae5483", "type": "concept", "title": "Hygiene, light, air, and minimum dwelling"}, {"id": "46507b05-f35f-41ae-a7da-d264a51cb408", "type": "concept", "title": "Functionalism's exclusions and simplifications"}], "description": "Students examine claims that architecture should express use, structure, economy, and social need."}, {"id": "55c1666b-4ed8-4abe-b3b2-e845d6fb200c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Le Corbusier, Mies, and Modernist Authority", "children": [{"id": "f2e7321f-8af9-476d-980d-466f5db1a536", "type": "concept", "title": "Towards a New Architecture"}, {"id": "b2fb60fd-8402-44a5-b1a0-bc7b5378ac3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Five points, standardization, and the machine aesthetic"}, {"id": "d407fe57-b85b-46db-bc23-0d4797904010", "type": "concept", "title": "Mies, universal space, and disciplined abstraction"}, {"id": "dc2f9299-4fb6-4718-81d7-dd26fbf4d0ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon, charisma, and institutional reception"}], "description": "This subtopic studies canonical modernist texts as arguments, images, and self-mythologies."}, {"id": "616dd95e-838a-4ae3-a059-780f4cdb95a6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Reform and Utopia", "children": [{"id": "4b8e098b-9f18-41b2-9ea3-4ebb0b789760", "type": "concept", "title": "CIAM and the functional city"}, {"id": "3f26727f-e31b-4992-a24b-b8aec4ea2212", "type": "concept", "title": "Bauhaus pedagogy and industrial design"}, {"id": "2d93e176-efbf-4044-bbcf-eee601ecf97b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective life and mass housing"}, {"id": "c561f447-4967-4b61-af71-77b376ea0191", "type": "concept", "title": "Utopia, control, and lived experience"}], "description": "Students connect modernist theory to housing, planning, education, and the promise of social transformation."}]}, {"id": "a22b88aa-8002-4cba-82d8-6b1bf8010903", "type": "topic", "title": "Phenomenology, Embodiment, Atmosphere, and Place", "description": "Phenomenology shifts theory from visual composition to embodied experience. Students study body, memory, material, atmosphere, and place as central architectural concerns.", "children": [{"id": "a4bfa92a-626d-4e7e-a872-63639eac46f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodied Perception", "children": [{"id": "818c9c7c-46e8-43b7-926d-45194347a7d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Pallasmaa and the critique of ocularcentrism"}, {"id": "0f31c141-ce2f-4fbb-bb84-cc33d91f859e", "type": "concept", "title": "Touch, sound, smell, movement, and scale"}, {"id": "0431d5fc-23aa-4593-a300-46ce4c2197f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Hapticity, memory, and material presence"}, {"id": "27d6dc1e-4f19-4f4a-94b1-e844b19e6e07", "type": "concept", "title": "The body as measure and participant"}], "description": "Students learn why experience is multisensory, temporal, and bodily."}, {"id": "866be65d-11fa-488b-adc2-1bb08d019e0c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Place, Dwelling, and Meaning", "children": [{"id": "932c0dd6-2042-4dfd-946b-b33ec7b3e327", "type": "concept", "title": "Heidegger, Norberg-Schulz, and dwelling"}, {"id": "41fd88df-7f40-4f77-9de3-e725b475a9d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Genius loci and place-making"}, {"id": "8d6e26e8-771d-4f37-b941-9993d796551b", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, threshold, landscape, and ritual"}, {"id": "c98d72e2-723d-4cc8-b240-1bb7982129a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of nostalgia and essentialism"}], "description": "This subtopic examines architecture as a way of orienting human existence in the world."}, {"id": "4cb0e0e6-e632-4340-babf-1617f3505778", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Atmosphere and Material Poetics", "children": [{"id": "3e81deb2-1332-41ac-90b6-847ca016b754", "type": "concept", "title": "Zumthor and atmospheres"}, {"id": "05f2882b-4571-4a6d-b7fc-1c9606ffa346", "type": "concept", "title": "Light, shadow, temperature, texture, and silence"}, {"id": "5e9988d9-5858-4f93-96f4-f062e74e997b", "type": "concept", "title": "Aging, patina, and weathering"}, {"id": "dc50dd7f-847e-49ab-b9bd-1da16000c933", "type": "concept", "title": "Phenomenology in design critique"}], "description": "Students study how materials, light, detail, weather, and time produce affective environments."}]}, {"id": "034544f9-ade6-4125-8320-8a47a3b96008", "type": "topic", "title": "Semiotics, Meaning, Pop Culture, and Architectural Communication", "description": "This unit treats architecture as sign, language, image, and communication. Students learn how buildings signify through form, ornament, context, media, and use.", "children": [{"id": "a173c203-65b0-4fcf-9997-6ef45612d000", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as Sign System", "children": [{"id": "94778081-ab3f-4e67-9982-4c095091b3dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Signifier, signified, code, and convention"}, {"id": "7c4cb6d8-db90-4d45-8fab-4d40c8bff5d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Icon, index, symbol, and architectural meaning"}, {"id": "69973b4a-e167-4bc6-bf12-992d35adc914", "type": "concept", "title": "Language analogies and their limits"}, {"id": "b6a08c29-a7e0-4bb8-b1fd-ee521cbdf51f", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning as social and historical"}], "description": "Students examine semiotic approaches that read buildings as communicative systems."}, {"id": "90ba88a0-dffb-4504-b536-fb55de3da9e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Popular Culture and the Ordinary", "children": [{"id": "4bad2e7b-8803-4037-9cad-78a46b185037", "type": "concept", "title": "Learning from Las Vegas"}, {"id": "6dcdbb67-0b89-4a8e-97ef-a132b3d52849", "type": "concept", "title": "Ducks and decorated sheds"}, {"id": "cc4b143a-6c30-4684-ba17-302e88909469", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial strips, signs, and roadside urbanism"}, {"id": "889d80cc-afb0-4983-841b-3005c8e27be1", "type": "concept", "title": "High culture, low culture, and disciplinary anxiety"}], "description": "Postmodern theory took ordinary landscapes seriously as sources of communication and meaning."}, {"id": "4dca60af-765a-485d-bd4d-e9d1c5c9db82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and Reception", "children": [{"id": "7031e6ed-c021-48ba-8ec3-ec32b5c0582c", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, interpretation, and ambiguity"}, {"id": "bf297745-06ce-4344-9f75-e719820f0c1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Branding, image, and spectacle"}, {"id": "69bee12e-6db2-4ba7-b2d0-53327f9a5278", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions and media reception"}, {"id": "f34dbf7d-3b7d-4c5b-8c02-4ef1a36d787b", "type": "concept", "title": "Misreading, appropriation, and popular use"}], "description": "Students learn that meaning is produced through users, critics, photographs, publications, and institutions."}]}, {"id": "4d928244-f58a-46b8-b2cb-b0c31268c78c", "type": "topic", "title": "Postmodernism, Complexity, Contradiction, and Historical Reference", "description": "This section studies postmodernism as a critique of modernist purity and a re-opening of history, symbolism, irony, urban context, and pluralism.", "children": [{"id": "f9429243-998e-4b8d-be84-a9bf9ebee558", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Venturi and Contradiction", "children": [{"id": "a2a4bac2-3f33-44d2-8a98-534c61338b72", "type": "concept", "title": "Both-and rather than either-or"}, {"id": "58f46f7d-12d3-4191-9b0e-ea00623055a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, richness, and difficult unity"}, {"id": "c3b55e46-992c-412f-9634-a957d379400d", "type": "concept", "title": "Main Street and ordinary architecture"}, {"id": "5841580a-9ae9-404d-b145-7f3b71c8d8e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Contradiction as design method"}], "description": "Students analyze complexity as a theoretical challenge to reductive modernism."}, {"id": "d5b91e89-ad39-41c5-9de6-6fc4487390f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History, Typology, and Memory", "children": [{"id": "cac090d6-94be-4afd-ab3f-a8e7cad79385", "type": "concept", "title": "Rossi, collective memory, and the city"}, {"id": "1d327217-222b-4dc8-b463-3f3950b5c984", "type": "concept", "title": "Typology, monument, and permanence"}, {"id": "1cacf0e9-ace1-4af3-815e-56906d5c8435", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextualism and urban morphology"}, {"id": "2ab2bfd7-b77a-4819-9490-a905a709e82c", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastiche, quotation, and irony"}], "description": "Postmodern theory revived historical reference and urban continuity as theoretical concerns."}, {"id": "a14ec7a5-a1cc-4c8d-b6ab-d08e3a577c2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pluralism and Its Critiques", "children": [{"id": "dabd3832-8e95-486f-a3d9-621c7fd781c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Plural meaning and cultural difference"}, {"id": "756d45c5-4f56-4625-9ee0-6f7698196040", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial spectacle and surface"}, {"id": "4e311d48-a683-40a9-906d-9db256ff419c", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity, representation, and inclusion"}, {"id": "d6fb728d-0a52-4a60-b52b-a706df27bd68", "type": "concept", "title": "Postmodernism after postmodernism"}], "description": "Students evaluate postmodernism's liberating and problematic dimensions."}]}, {"id": "0a77e579-4a9c-4c29-8560-792adcd0f279", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Theory, Marxism, Ideology, and Architectural Production", "description": "This section connects architecture to social power. Students study how buildings and cities reproduce, resist, or reveal capitalism, ideology, class, labor, institutions, and cultural authority.", "children": [{"id": "f2434d0d-b893-4455-becd-529876b9cacb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marxist and Political-Economic Readings", "children": [{"id": "ad3492aa-ba3f-492c-af5f-bc4e8143c8c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Use value, exchange value, and real estate"}, {"id": "e204e81d-aa4f-457b-9f73-f236afd15bdf", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodification of space"}, {"id": "7fa259d5-6731-4935-a0bf-10ca615a8af0", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, construction, and invisible work"}, {"id": "67ee30a3-0925-4f14-9c0b-7b530c4ac071", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, rent, and development pressure"}], "description": "Students learn to read architecture through production, labor, land, value, and capital."}, {"id": "42d4a1e4-9420-4327-be30-21a953c93ecd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology, Institutions, and Culture", "children": [{"id": "5dc56293-19a2-4fdb-ba49-ef8c91cbfe04", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and cultural production"}, {"id": "6f5ba4fc-aa75-4426-a688-752173853fd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, schools, awards, and publishing"}, {"id": "e2472e06-34d2-4937-986d-a2c06d51e206", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional norms and disciplinary gatekeeping"}, {"id": "a645f437-c27f-470b-a4a9-d88c81124f4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique of neutrality"}], "description": "Architecture participates in making authority appear natural, beautiful, or inevitable."}, {"id": "6cab0bc9-d209-4a3a-945d-409535ef5be2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical Practice", "children": [{"id": "999665d3-a3ab-40ef-baac-f0e7140818aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy versus instrumentality"}, {"id": "9064ba73-8529-496a-b0f3-2d8b85532dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Activism, advocacy, and community design"}, {"id": "f2c0cfa8-cbf2-4d12-acb7-8656e43082eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional critique"}, {"id": "b7912f25-eade-4c00-a2a6-9014f66c25d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of critical formalism"}], "description": "Students ask whether architecture can be politically critical through design, practice, research, or refusal."}]}, {"id": "b2942945-af79-4a26-9ba2-87b9a5d81c13", "type": "topic", "title": "Deconstruction, Disjunction, Event, Diagram, and Late Theory", "description": "This unit studies late twentieth-century theory as architecture engaged philosophy, language, instability, event, and media. Students learn both its conceptual power and its institutional excesses.", "children": [{"id": "8b252071-2b84-4465-a1b6-d45c314e030b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Deconstruction and Architectural Form", "children": [{"id": "1eded5a1-1dbc-498a-a853-3d78e2014729", "type": "concept", "title": "Derrida, Eisenman, Tschumi, Libeskind, and Hadid"}, {"id": "4a526cb8-bcab-4637-b6eb-751252486e2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Text, trace, supplement, and undecidability"}, {"id": "7af0403d-86db-4bd3-b1f0-bf8c12fd462e", "type": "concept", "title": "MoMA Deconstructivist Architecture exhibition"}, {"id": "d9d282e3-d8ab-434e-991b-a61267588fa3", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, philosophy, and spectacle"}], "description": "Students examine fragmentation, trace, absence, and instability in theory and design."}, {"id": "d35a57f4-71ba-4df0-a0ce-460319166c18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Event, Program, and Disjunction", "children": [{"id": "92499c66-a64d-424a-9b22-5b615b69f315", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture and Disjunction"}, {"id": "aa3fee75-beeb-4770-8ac0-a9ce46da1e3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Event-space, program, movement, and sequence"}, {"id": "4c19085b-b125-4cad-a0f6-d481f5a04fe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-programming and montage"}, {"id": "873fdc45-8fe6-4d83-9279-61f240f4d2ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Parc de la Villette as theoretical project"}], "description": "Tschumi and others challenged the idea of architecture as stable form alone."}, {"id": "536912d8-c3bc-4d88-a7d0-0e2f121076e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diagram, Index, and Late-Theory Institutions", "children": [{"id": "59d3b4cd-b9c4-4970-a3d9-42cb5e498631", "type": "concept", "title": "Diagram as generator and evidence"}, {"id": "44d41c6d-72fe-40ad-8601-a94e5272e063", "type": "concept", "title": "Journals, schools, exhibitions, and conferences"}, {"id": "3345849c-487b-4b88-bdba-e5ec2082571c", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory culture and studio pedagogy"}, {"id": "e6c39ffc-1ac8-4d68-bb55-9fbb52f9a036", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of opacity and jargon"}], "description": "Students learn how diagrams and academic networks shaped late twentieth-century discourse."}]}, {"id": "dd087251-8627-4601-966c-d6c14b38e2b8", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Race, Labor, Authorship, and the Limits of the Canon", "description": "This section challenges the architectural canon by asking who gets to theorize, design, build, inhabit, and be remembered. Students connect theory to inequity in authorship, representation, labor, and space.", "children": [{"id": "04a4bb9e-4a98-4097-9694-872c727aaa05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist and Queer Architectural Theory", "children": [{"id": "800206a2-7ede-430d-b388-7940448805e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Domesticity, care, and reproductive labor"}, {"id": "2b76244c-935c-4181-9e28-f765b546e006", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist critiques of authorship and studio culture"}, {"id": "7ab8be5b-d08e-4fc1-9f28-15d4471ab573", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer space and non-normative publics"}, {"id": "b5e44132-17df-45ab-bc09-30d02504095b", "type": "concept", "title": "Bodies, bathrooms, safety, and access"}], "description": "Students examine how gender and sexuality reshape spatial analysis and professional history."}, {"id": "270f9370-9cb0-49c6-a996-7f4272447f31", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Modernity, and Spatial Power", "children": [{"id": "c038dbb9-59a9-4680-a87a-9c0fb7e0a1ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and Modern Architecture"}, {"id": "d534a994-61e9-4561-8201-b3ebdf3283c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Redlining, urban renewal, and spatial segregation"}, {"id": "d4cb8359-3eb9-46fc-a900-3702ae6bb2ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Black, Indigenous, and minority architects and theorists"}, {"id": "754b47b3-1aa7-46af-9521-15d423a3978b", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memory, and spatial justice"}], "description": "This subtopic connects architecture to racial capitalism, coloniality, segregation, and representation."}, {"id": "d43f17ce-8a17-4b50-a9e6-588ff63cd4f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor, Credit, and Collective Production", "children": [{"id": "9c8de9fd-5719-46ea-a5d3-55213bd56baf", "type": "concept", "title": "Office labor and invisible collaborators"}, {"id": "77f815cf-5ba9-4c61-9658-efd848aaca43", "type": "concept", "title": "Construction labor and material supply chains"}, {"id": "b17273a4-18eb-477b-b839-2ae44e714570", "type": "concept", "title": "Credit, archives, and intellectual property"}, {"id": "aa1b612c-1874-4238-b1aa-068f66c4bda4", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship beyond the master architect"}], "description": "Students learn that architecture is produced collectively, even when theory celebrates individual authors."}]}, {"id": "4c058d43-7f07-47b6-bfa7-416ca47376a8", "type": "topic", "title": "Globalization, Colonialism, Decolonial Critique, and Comparative Theory", "description": "This unit expands architectural theory beyond Euro-American universals. Students learn to read theory through translation, empire, migration, climate, development, and situated knowledge.", "children": [{"id": "5c273502-2c88-4f2e-b02e-aaef4ab9df01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial and Imperial Architectural Knowledge", "children": [{"id": "e2f7eb56-8a98-4e77-acd8-77e1d3fb2ac3", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial planning and administrative architecture"}, {"id": "edc12635-891a-4e37-8c92-8ce5cda5b2aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionary, educational, and extractive spaces"}, {"id": "c82e9315-582c-4de8-a813-022ea22dd203", "type": "concept", "title": "Tropical modernism and climatic expertise"}, {"id": "0d7c94f9-b6ec-4012-ace8-6041b6c16c1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, surveys, and knowledge production"}], "description": "Students examine how architectural theory traveled through unequal power relations."}, {"id": "24f726ec-2f7d-48a8-82a2-87699e9f9b1c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Modernisms and Translation", "children": [{"id": "e55843bc-263e-4c94-9f97-abe1eff57ace", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American, African, South Asian, East Asian, and Middle Eastern modernisms"}, {"id": "1cf05cd3-5b53-4b13-b8b8-c520a1318270", "type": "concept", "title": "Nation-building and postcolonial institutions"}, {"id": "9a61c33e-0ff5-460a-9b59-1cdc65e9fa0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Local materials, climate, and labor"}, {"id": "063509ef-4bf5-4875-a8f5-4acfd2c255a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation, hybridity, and misrecognition"}], "description": "This subtopic studies modern architecture as multiple, situated, and contested rather than simply exported."}, {"id": "7efeba50-ba1c-4e31-8cb6-41ecbbad48be", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decolonial and Comparative Theory", "children": [{"id": "2027e513-613e-48da-b617-d2b5b16311d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Decolonial critique and epistemic justice"}, {"id": "66dac322-4d01-422b-adb8-04fd731ef420", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous spatial knowledge and sovereignty"}, {"id": "9d2d3780-9dde-4684-aacd-968680269112", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative method and its risks"}, {"id": "a9c5f28a-5c6f-46ec-a4b7-9a7e48cfed75", "type": "concept", "title": "Against tokenism and essentialism"}], "description": "Students develop methods for theory that do not treat the West as default center."}]}, {"id": "d22ae319-9505-4bc9-86a6-7d44b2c72d07", "type": "topic", "title": "Urban Theory, Density, Everyday Life, Housing, and Public Space", "description": "This section connects architectural theory to urban life. Students study streets, housing, infrastructure, public space, density, informality, and everyday use as theoretical problems.", "children": [{"id": "62a31b2d-a90c-46b5-a097-2103bee38036", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The City as Theoretical Object", "children": [{"id": "67dc889a-d87a-4b2e-b190-3b950e0dea59", "type": "concept", "title": "Simmel, Benjamin, Lefebvre, Jacobs, Koolhaas, and others"}, {"id": "af9d5ce6-6633-4031-8621-d3dbc9b0be46", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space, crowds, and everyday life"}, {"id": "39432ef0-128a-4402-b914-916533520d72", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure and metropolitan systems"}, {"id": "53163425-9e97-4988-9d47-6ae42ac9006c", "type": "concept", "title": "The city as text, machine, and ecosystem"}], "description": "Students learn to theorize the city through morphology, infrastructure, economy, governance, and experience."}, {"id": "1c73edad-6e96-4062-b0d0-e6ded712ef9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Housing and Social Reproduction", "children": [{"id": "05f060bd-34e6-41af-9478-1246251a5de2", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum dwelling and mass housing"}, {"id": "f74a6a9d-4cc6-4993-9443-bd543a0a407f", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic labor, care, and family forms"}, {"id": "b1caee41-cc9f-413c-ba60-e7734a3e50b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Affordability, displacement, and homelessness"}, {"id": "a7f0e866-5e1f-4c2d-8f07-b62df82ea9b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Cooperative, informal, and community-led housing"}], "description": "Architecture theory becomes concrete in the politics of housing."}, {"id": "edec3e31-c033-45d8-817a-435375687168", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Publics, Protest, and Spatial Justice", "children": [{"id": "1fbd2454-f36b-453b-9aba-7fa288bdc116", "type": "concept", "title": "Squares, streets, parks, campuses, and civic interiors"}, {"id": "81c7609c-cd9b-4795-99fd-2f18abd7c3e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Protest, policing, surveillance, and assembly"}, {"id": "e9bc6e08-91de-444d-858e-27958ae6ca14", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility, safety, and exclusion"}, {"id": "4bf3295e-5ed4-4ca0-b54d-300f601b32ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Informality, repair, and commoning"}], "description": "Students study public space as contested political terrain."}]}, {"id": "866f0c5e-9053-4fc1-a776-64fa902d6b77", "type": "topic", "title": "Media, Representation, Visual Culture, and Digital Theory", "description": "This unit studies the media through which architecture is imagined, circulated, judged, and monetized. Students learn that representation does not merely depict architecture; it shapes architectural reality.", "children": [{"id": "39dc64f3-9eb1-42f1-962d-bb32ec142fbe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drawing, Photography, and Exhibition", "children": [{"id": "6b24c5f7-13d8-4c1a-9ccb-521bfe9b0542", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan, section, axonometric, collage, and diagram"}, {"id": "5c935d9f-b719-41a4-9fe7-e996fc66af60", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and the modern canon"}, {"id": "2554ba52-78bc-411e-9af1-aedd3c8560f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions, biennales, and curatorial argument"}, {"id": "76abf08b-0cb5-4a7b-82b4-8acc972d5f6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, collections, and institutional framing"}], "description": "Students examine representational media as theoretical instruments."}, {"id": "a09f4c6b-0f05-4be0-a871-b13658fd1348", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual Culture and Spectacle", "children": [{"id": "6042a2a3-6488-4e65-b973-2375af4a1ef4", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconicity and city branding"}, {"id": "0081451c-6739-46d8-86a3-fe105c148447", "type": "concept", "title": "Starchitects and global image economies"}, {"id": "91fe3d96-b151-40b4-b4a9-90dcc6e2b0f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Magazines, websites, renders, and social media"}, {"id": "fdbcc800-51e2-41d1-b099-bbe0df7b050b", "type": "concept", "title": "Spectacle versus lived experience"}], "description": "Architecture operates in a culture of images, attention, branding, and tourism."}, {"id": "b1809ec9-bcd0-414d-97f6-c14544e8c942", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital Theory and Computational Culture", "children": [{"id": "9d99dc21-2e9d-4a2a-81ac-6e67f5727b45", "type": "concept", "title": "Parametricism and algorithmic design"}, {"id": "25746684-e37b-4fb8-9b49-bd43d9a7985b", "type": "concept", "title": "BIM, platforms, automation, and data"}, {"id": "b1533151-a6bb-4588-ad6f-6e3f40ef21f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital fabrication and material intelligence"}, {"id": "1225dfbe-640b-45dc-a535-95171c91b56c", "type": "concept", "title": "AI, authorship, and design judgment"}], "description": "Students assess how computation changes form, authorship, labor, and publics."}]}, {"id": "ac72e1d9-dedd-4624-8e3c-7dfe16355e2b", "type": "topic", "title": "Ecology, Technology, Materiality, and Posthuman Debates", "description": "This section addresses theory under climate crisis. Students examine architecture as part of energy systems, material flows, nonhuman agencies, technologies, and planetary responsibilities.", "children": [{"id": "3a06f480-68a9-44d1-a059-7da4b53a32eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecological Thought and Climate Theory", "children": [{"id": "a5b85fc4-f309-45a4-9491-91f058ec3e1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Sustainability, resilience, adaptation, and mitigation"}, {"id": "dbdfa065-47b8-442d-a2e5-4f54d1be42c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, carbon, and operational systems"}, {"id": "091661e2-6a8d-414d-9e56-441ce49ae95f", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental justice and uneven vulnerability"}, {"id": "6f09a069-2eaa-4a7e-9c11-9c8b65471d47", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture after growth and extraction"}], "description": "Students learn how climate changes the object and ethics of architecture."}, {"id": "7bab5adb-54ff-409f-ae33-5c7fdd98762a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Materiality and Agency", "children": [{"id": "2ef763dc-b3cb-4acc-b9d2-d66523ee4631", "type": "concept", "title": "Matter, weathering, maintenance, and decay"}, {"id": "b49d1b19-4ff2-4a3c-90fb-d3eaee3716af", "type": "concept", "title": "New materialism and material agency"}, {"id": "03c833f4-ebdc-44fb-97a9-d2234bccc498", "type": "concept", "title": "Toxicity, waste, and embodied carbon"}, {"id": "0ea94a6d-ab48-4b5d-9b81-f7c47f8af0f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Reuse, repair, and circularity"}], "description": "This subtopic treats materials as active participants in architectural life."}, {"id": "9cd606a8-94b9-4e30-8583-e072a92f76ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, Infrastructure, and Posthuman Questions", "children": [{"id": "a51c8080-be1d-4b13-9542-4229e5c2542b", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure, logistics, and planetary urbanization"}, {"id": "669ec03c-439c-45cc-b28b-7128bd0d37fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Smart buildings, sensors, and surveillance"}, {"id": "9749f911-8714-43d9-abdc-1ec10d61279d", "type": "concept", "title": "Animals, plants, microbes, and multispecies design"}, {"id": "7150b4a5-99ea-4cd4-8ca2-13173fe21c3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Posthuman ethics and design limits"}], "description": "Students ask how architectural theory changes when humans are not the only actors."}]}, {"id": "ca034601-2434-4142-a57b-a9012bcacc1f", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Debates: Practice, Ethics, Crisis, Preservation, and Futures", "description": "This unit gathers live debates so students can position themselves critically. The aim is to connect theory to professional, public, and design decisions under real constraints.", "children": [{"id": "cbad1846-578e-4e68-b997-de0cf54ab513", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Practice and Ethics", "children": [{"id": "0ac4fe02-64bb-4116-8520-d608ca60dc2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Client, public, and planetary responsibilities"}, {"id": "a0e66a35-ebd4-42a4-a53d-2e623e671a77", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, procurement, and fee structures"}, {"id": "8e13fcc8-c90a-4183-8437-34607fa1e314", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, expertise, and accountability"}, {"id": "0e6097fb-c4fe-4df8-8a1b-93fb7663965d", "type": "concept", "title": "Refusal, advocacy, and alternative practice models"}], "description": "Students examine how theoretical commitments become professional choices."}, {"id": "002b1646-9d6d-4716-a7bd-4bbd845f806c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Crisis, Preservation, and Adaptation", "children": [{"id": "0875f6d8-6ea4-4eec-9aab-fe7459f62821", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptive reuse and retrofit"}, {"id": "a011a1bb-5abb-4243-8c95-82128c5dd207", "type": "concept", "title": "Preservation, memory, and contested heritage"}, {"id": "65509aef-b2e2-483e-8aac-9e3f95caa892", "type": "concept", "title": "Disaster, migration, and temporary shelter"}, {"id": "2b5216e6-68d8-46e1-92e0-40b3329b5eb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Repair as design theory"}], "description": "Contemporary theory must address existing buildings, damaged worlds, and limited resources."}, {"id": "ab86d9a3-bebe-44ca-888c-3e352d2744d4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Futures of the Discipline", "children": [{"id": "00819711-3100-41a6-9784-8b74a6c6ee2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate-constrained design culture"}, {"id": "35a18db9-9205-4a41-a8c2-19a786aa44c8", "type": "concept", "title": "AI, automation, and authorship"}, {"id": "d5452f8c-52db-4abd-85a3-30907b13fe82", "type": "concept", "title": "Post-professional and interdisciplinary practice"}, {"id": "ad9f8691-8ec1-4b9f-977b-b78dcecb51fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as future-making and critique"}], "description": "Students speculate responsibly about what architecture can become."}]}, {"id": "ad078920-93a8-4a53-8fdf-e7da8b3e9ee6", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory Research Lab: Close Reading, Argument, and Design Translation", "description": "The capstone section turns theory into scholarly and design work. Students learn to read closely, argue clearly, facilitate discussion, and translate theory into a design position without flattening it into slogans.", "children": [{"id": "eff6639d-cc3b-441e-81cf-dbc1bf78fef6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close Reading and Concept Mapping", "children": [{"id": "145ec4ff-ade4-4f69-a043-13f331b2003e", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis, method, evidence, and audience"}, {"id": "5f71a626-4291-4b65-b97f-6c31817c9dbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and conceptual genealogies"}, {"id": "01daf74b-d39d-4898-9912-c7f6b59f0473", "type": "concept", "title": "Productive confusion and seminar questions"}, {"id": "7bd8af55-d04e-4cb6-91a9-de6df3c938ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing texts without false equivalence"}], "description": "Students practice reading difficult texts by extracting terms, claims, evidence, stakes, and contradictions."}, {"id": "294005af-e69a-41f8-9408-73cc278a7ea0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research Methods and Writing", "children": [{"id": "5008093a-f10d-469e-9553-0e1f5c72b94d", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question and historiographic context"}, {"id": "f1dff4fa-92e9-4ba0-8eaf-18461123ca53", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary texts, archives, visual evidence, and secondary scholarship"}, {"id": "3b50f299-6102-4b58-a777-ba33b813af61", "type": "concept", "title": "Abstracts, annotated bibliography, and outline"}, {"id": "634943aa-cdf5-4287-bb91-ca3738330d7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Drafting, peer review, revision, and citation"}], "description": "This subtopic builds a paper from sources, buildings, images, and arguments."}, {"id": "0c12376f-ef7d-4a1e-9040-017f1ff1df59", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Design-Theory Translation", "children": [{"id": "2760a2ce-faf1-4956-b225-e69a3271bed1", "type": "concept", "title": "Design position statement"}, {"id": "bdee7370-7bd3-48b5-ad4c-9658506dfe97", "type": "concept", "title": "Precedent analysis through theory"}, {"id": "2d480056-aab5-4535-bc58-4a4823105789", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique language and studio application"}, {"id": "d59c7102-0d08-447e-8c2d-36623e19a80d", "type": "concept", "title": "Final seminar presentation and reflection"}], "description": "Students learn to connect theory to design practice with rigor and restraint."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$tharjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'theory-of-architecture'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Theory of Architecture',
  goal = 'Theory of Architecture',
  summary = 'Theory of Architecture is an advanced seminar in architectural discourse, criticism, and design thought. Students read foundational and contemporary texts alongside buildings, cities, drawings, exhibitions, and media. The course develops the ability to interpret architecture as cultural argument, political practice, sensory experience, technical imagination, historical construction, and public responsibility.',
  data = $tharjson${"slug": "theory-of-architecture", "title": "Theory of Architecture", "description": "Theory of Architecture is an advanced seminar in architectural discourse, criticism, and design thought. Students read foundational and contemporary texts alongside buildings, cities, drawings, exhibitions, and media. The course develops the ability to interpret architecture as cultural argument, political practice, sensory experience, technical imagination, historical construction, and public responsibility.", "topics": [{"id": "88710dd6-feae-402d-8c55-fd06b300e28d", "type": "topic", "title": "What Is Architectural Theory?", "description": "This opening section frames theory as a disciplined way of asking what architecture is, what it does, who it serves, and how it can be judged. Students learn that theory is not an ornament added to design; it is a set of tools for seeing assumptions.", "children": [{"id": "9fd2253b-fd9a-4a68-9975-b2b2a4c3a7aa", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Theory as Question and Method", "children": [{"id": "bd0e020e-a025-4b4d-afdc-8790ab4a45e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Concepts, claims, evidence, and critique"}, {"id": "a0ffbf9c-2dd8-4e12-a1ea-dd37fa73c862", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory versus history, criticism, and design method"}, {"id": "12409c26-7529-40f5-9d56-c9c7a5063c15", "type": "concept", "title": "Normative, descriptive, speculative, and political theory"}, {"id": "e67d1255-ec23-43f1-b7fc-8d395b94401b", "type": "concept", "title": "How theory changes what designers notice"}], "description": "Students define theory as a way of formulating questions, concepts, and arguments about buildings and cities."}, {"id": "3da0181e-4de3-4df9-a8d2-9136d166c47a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as Discipline and Practice", "children": [{"id": "58ed0743-f77c-422f-be58-eccffef6f259", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture, building, urbanism, and landscape"}, {"id": "32a8dad7-b6cc-45ae-9c95-39f348824714", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship, expertise, and institutional authority"}, {"id": "4f8ec3c1-49de-43d7-94c6-1d81395c3487", "type": "concept", "title": "Studio culture and disciplinary reproduction"}, {"id": "d526575d-1678-49ab-b870-6c282f293bd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Practice, pedagogy, publication, and public debate"}], "description": "This subtopic asks how architecture became a profession, a discipline, and a cultural field."}, {"id": "e49e429a-86f9-4ed6-852d-15748e15387b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading Buildings and Texts Together", "children": [{"id": "c2273ea5-b076-443c-a9bf-17c84624f493", "type": "concept", "title": "Close reading of texts"}, {"id": "baece6f6-37de-41e9-b1f1-cf46276853d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Formal, spatial, material, and urban analysis"}, {"id": "c715b985-335b-42e0-9b16-d8196f699bc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Manifestoes, treatises, criticism, and interviews"}, {"id": "f3381c17-3f39-4a7e-b453-0870276c81f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Using theory to frame a design position"}], "description": "Students learn to move between written theory and spatial evidence without reducing one to the other."}]}, {"id": "6a67a339-8eb4-4cfa-8df1-dd926d52799f", "type": "topic", "title": "Vitruvius, Classical Theory, and the Origins of Architectural Discourse", "description": "This section introduces classical architectural theory as a durable source of vocabulary, hierarchy, proportion, and professional identity. The point is not to revere the canon, but to understand how architectural authority was constructed.", "children": [{"id": "a495b883-851d-4179-b73a-979751177347", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Vitruvian Categories and Professional Identity", "children": [{"id": "7fcffe97-7c43-4ac8-bc49-1082e040022d", "type": "concept", "title": "Firmitas, utilitas, venustas"}, {"id": "57dc28a0-3f56-4447-af93-0af1a01c44cb", "type": "concept", "title": "The architect's education and breadth of knowledge"}, {"id": "7620ac19-ec49-4aeb-af27-cac6b34faf73", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodity, firmness, delight, and later translations"}, {"id": "d39d4689-3352-43fa-97ab-0cde7da8320d", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, public works, and civic responsibility"}], "description": "Students examine how Vitruvius linked architecture to knowledge, ethics, public life, and the body."}, {"id": "3b5bcbb0-186f-4e09-93c3-75dcb49622b9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Orders, Proportion, and the Body", "children": [{"id": "53b2d7c0-8c0f-4cc5-8eb3-4b17ae654ac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Column orders and decorum"}, {"id": "b5598f88-615b-43f2-babd-e08a319e988c", "type": "concept", "title": "Anthropometry, symmetry, and harmony"}, {"id": "6931a211-d887-42f1-ae83-4613532de9ad", "type": "concept", "title": "Temple, house, theater, and city as theoretical objects"}, {"id": "13689302-53b1-4d5d-9da9-692c4798e4f2", "type": "concept", "title": "Gendered and political assumptions in classical proportion"}], "description": "Classical theory makes proportion and the body central to architectural judgment."}, {"id": "28e67f9f-88e5-461a-b817-35c166a942b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Classical Theory's Afterlives", "children": [{"id": "a020c26e-4350-431f-b5f0-209e035b9c5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon formation and pedagogy"}, {"id": "0e248f20-aba5-4273-9c79-92c6cd9b54c8", "type": "concept", "title": "Classicism as universal language and political symbol"}, {"id": "aec805c5-72df-4c6c-b145-4171709bc01a", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial and state uses of classical imagery"}, {"id": "ffeccf3a-414e-427f-8663-63b7f62524ec", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique, parody, and reinterpretation"}], "description": "Students track how classical ideas return in Renaissance, Enlightenment, Beaux-Arts, modern, and postmodern discourse."}]}, {"id": "dfea18ea-b467-4c84-a275-ec201c90e2f7", "type": "topic", "title": "Renaissance Humanism: Alberti, Palladio, Proportion, and Treatises", "description": "This unit studies the Renaissance as a moment when architectural theory became textual, authorial, humanist, and print-based. Students learn how theory shaped design practice, professional identity, and historical revival.", "children": [{"id": "6a2830e5-7bdd-4ac4-8096-13916c5b223b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Alberti and Architecture as Liberal Art", "children": [{"id": "277bfaac-d867-4c30-80cc-4fcada5ada19", "type": "concept", "title": "Lineamenta and design as mental work"}, {"id": "f56cf93b-5b51-462a-a696-572af055ccb7", "type": "concept", "title": "Beauty, concinnitas, and proportion"}, {"id": "25cb268b-c35c-4209-a1b2-7668b38a06ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Patronage, city, and civic order"}, {"id": "4fe33f31-f0bb-4731-bd85-4648927b895a", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as professional elevation"}], "description": "Students study Alberti's claim that architecture belongs to intellectual culture, not only building craft."}, {"id": "a5435129-7a9a-46e5-8b1b-6688a43b2bf7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Palladio, Typology, and the Treatise", "children": [{"id": "c6723d12-a4e9-49e0-a9f6-b128f84606d2", "type": "concept", "title": "Villa, palace, church, and bridge types"}, {"id": "9a15617e-757b-4d9c-84ac-caebf3d6a108", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan, elevation, section, and measured precedent"}, {"id": "33acfa74-32f4-4550-8b34-67d9e573dc91", "type": "concept", "title": "Publication and dissemination of authority"}, {"id": "668753bd-3184-4da5-9d9d-4e9d85684f47", "type": "concept", "title": "Palladianism as transnational theory"}], "description": "Palladio shows how drawings, measurements, precedents, and books circulate architectural knowledge."}, {"id": "b32d2965-1963-4146-9d59-b3ab6f2c0ed4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Humanism, Perspective, and Representation", "children": [{"id": "ebf38862-2cbc-491a-99e3-6498c80da0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Perspective and visual order"}, {"id": "2f573f96-4c85-4c3a-a1ce-f561389fca37", "type": "concept", "title": "Architectural drawing as theoretical evidence"}, {"id": "32a36334-d3e3-4aaf-bcae-f5dbd8187930", "type": "concept", "title": "Human measure and ideal bodies"}, {"id": "f4980c0d-2ae7-46b7-81fd-021d16b9b29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of humanist universality"}], "description": "This subtopic links theory to drawing, vision, authorship, and the human subject."}]}, {"id": "bae05267-1a34-4fbc-9dce-11278452e809", "type": "topic", "title": "Enlightenment Theory: Taste, Sublime, Reason, Type, and Publics", "description": "This section explores theory as public argument about taste, reason, sensation, morality, institutions, and historical knowledge. Students see architecture as part of Enlightenment debates about society.", "children": [{"id": "4d33a72a-6a0b-4307-ad2e-5027f3dc2e38", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Taste, Judgment, and the Public Sphere", "children": [{"id": "22add8a6-dc48-41e6-9ee7-a5df8f64d64b", "type": "concept", "title": "Taste, beauty, judgment, and education"}, {"id": "4447dd5f-d21c-4452-a3fc-c705b6033c8f", "type": "concept", "title": "Criticism, academies, salons, and publics"}, {"id": "191d0592-c3c3-44db-a0f7-efc737c9f28b", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture as moral improvement"}, {"id": "52fd6eb9-8a3c-43f3-9634-7d5bd3bcb0d7", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal judgment versus cultural difference"}], "description": "Students examine how architecture became a matter of public criticism and cultivated judgment."}, {"id": "c700ac8d-1d7f-4499-af17-77ce2cc73d60", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The Sublime, Character, and Sensation", "children": [{"id": "0585f44b-dadc-44c5-96a1-88e40c76e8e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Burke, Kant, and aesthetic categories"}, {"id": "0dd56542-fb4b-4bc7-b7da-dc22cac39fc8", "type": "concept", "title": "Character, expression, and emotional effect"}, {"id": "aebcf430-fdea-47b0-b928-404c3124fab8", "type": "concept", "title": "Picturesque, ruin, and landscape"}, {"id": "2a38cc1f-e088-4e0c-a2d9-6e0c6fd51e59", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture as atmosphere and event"}], "description": "The sublime brought fear, scale, darkness, and affect into architectural discourse."}, {"id": "dae7e703-686b-419b-8365-e5ec77605807", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Type, History, and Rational Ordering", "children": [{"id": "6e17bc7a-f44e-470b-b721-62d4b845ae26", "type": "concept", "title": "Quatremere de Quincy and type"}, {"id": "e2d8c7ff-b3c4-4396-b0e8-006ec54d818e", "type": "concept", "title": "Ledoux, Boullee, and visionary institutions"}, {"id": "1d60cb8e-fb3d-452b-841c-cdfecb0542de", "type": "concept", "title": "Archaeology, historicism, and progress"}, {"id": "6614c26c-2f9a-4fec-b599-903a63af58de", "type": "concept", "title": "Prisons, museums, hospitals, schools, and civic reform"}], "description": "Students connect Enlightenment reason to classification, typology, and institutional design."}]}, {"id": "48c1c8c9-d5b2-49de-aaab-b11356bd6de0", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernity, Industrialization, and the Birth of the Manifesto", "description": "This unit links architectural theory to the social and technological shocks of industrial modernity. Students learn why the manifesto became a privileged modern form of architectural argument.", "children": [{"id": "8aa3ba66-5c7c-4c21-8eac-ffc0bdaa0fc5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Industry, Technology, and New Building Types", "children": [{"id": "7d801d7d-f8bb-4e82-a892-8c7d8e6be6e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Iron, glass, steel, reinforced concrete, and elevators"}, {"id": "d5de2ea3-f5c5-431e-9619-7a7702169108", "type": "concept", "title": "Engineering and architecture as rival authorities"}, {"id": "0a2a36e3-aa42-4b19-b6a9-5d38609b1e62", "type": "concept", "title": "Expositions and display culture"}, {"id": "84e63a28-3fa3-47dc-8aec-5bd3ebd8fc40", "type": "concept", "title": "The city as machine and network"}], "description": "Modern theory emerges from railways, factories, bridges, exhibitions, office towers, and infrastructure."}, {"id": "95837a92-1e6f-4ea9-90a9-37b7160ee4ee", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Manifestoes and Avant-Garde Rhetoric", "children": [{"id": "40fb3b77-d562-4df1-9d53-b8fb4edda070", "type": "concept", "title": "Futurist, Constructivist, De Stijl, and Bauhaus texts"}, {"id": "ae59ca4b-5e07-477f-8eed-503191cdebf8", "type": "concept", "title": "Polemic, brevity, image, and shock"}, {"id": "3a6e8ed6-fe49-4e40-9102-70f94d12e05f", "type": "concept", "title": "New man, new life, and social transformation"}, {"id": "a905f12d-cf8d-4daf-ba50-4f5f14f64876", "type": "concept", "title": "The risk of totalizing claims"}], "description": "Students learn how manifestoes declare enemies, futures, audiences, and design programs."}, {"id": "8af4176d-31c3-4e7a-b846-e18fc33544df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media and the Modern Architectural Public", "children": [{"id": "a87cb670-7661-4ef8-8977-ab666a48fcd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and canon formation"}, {"id": "fd8c941a-7595-499d-b2d9-2be9e3d5fc8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Architectural journals and international networks"}, {"id": "5d3acfc3-c48b-4277-be82-3802239defe9", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions as theory-making machines"}, {"id": "145d9e24-c8ef-43db-998a-ac6f3fcb5766", "type": "concept", "title": "Design as public persuasion"}], "description": "Journals, photographs, exhibitions, and books made modern architecture a discursive movement."}]}, {"id": "d7e2ee16-08da-4418-a547-fb50e8e7c407", "type": "topic", "title": "Modernist Theory: Function, Form, Social Reform, and the Avant-Garde", "description": "This section studies modernist theory as multiple, conflicted, and political. Students compare functionalism, social reform, abstraction, standardization, and technological optimism.", "children": [{"id": "f96311b5-dc67-487c-9802-22b1a67d114e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Functionalism and the New Objectivity", "children": [{"id": "1cce34f6-6c3b-4860-b5e9-203ece691d29", "type": "concept", "title": "Form follows function and its interpretations"}, {"id": "69f50cf4-8eec-4260-a990-84eaef956465", "type": "concept", "title": "Neue Sachlichkeit and rational design"}, {"id": "d695cf0c-1c24-427b-8fca-4d8b00ae5483", "type": "concept", "title": "Hygiene, light, air, and minimum dwelling"}, {"id": "46507b05-f35f-41ae-a7da-d264a51cb408", "type": "concept", "title": "Functionalism's exclusions and simplifications"}], "description": "Students examine claims that architecture should express use, structure, economy, and social need."}, {"id": "55c1666b-4ed8-4abe-b3b2-e845d6fb200c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Le Corbusier, Mies, and Modernist Authority", "children": [{"id": "f2e7321f-8af9-476d-980d-466f5db1a536", "type": "concept", "title": "Towards a New Architecture"}, {"id": "b2fb60fd-8402-44a5-b1a0-bc7b5378ac3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Five points, standardization, and the machine aesthetic"}, {"id": "d407fe57-b85b-46db-bc23-0d4797904010", "type": "concept", "title": "Mies, universal space, and disciplined abstraction"}, {"id": "dc2f9299-4fb6-4718-81d7-dd26fbf4d0ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Canon, charisma, and institutional reception"}], "description": "This subtopic studies canonical modernist texts as arguments, images, and self-mythologies."}, {"id": "616dd95e-838a-4ae3-a059-780f4cdb95a6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Reform and Utopia", "children": [{"id": "4b8e098b-9f18-41b2-9ea3-4ebb0b789760", "type": "concept", "title": "CIAM and the functional city"}, {"id": "3f26727f-e31b-4992-a24b-b8aec4ea2212", "type": "concept", "title": "Bauhaus pedagogy and industrial design"}, {"id": "2d93e176-efbf-4044-bbcf-eee601ecf97b", "type": "concept", "title": "Collective life and mass housing"}, {"id": "c561f447-4967-4b61-af71-77b376ea0191", "type": "concept", "title": "Utopia, control, and lived experience"}], "description": "Students connect modernist theory to housing, planning, education, and the promise of social transformation."}]}, {"id": "a22b88aa-8002-4cba-82d8-6b1bf8010903", "type": "topic", "title": "Phenomenology, Embodiment, Atmosphere, and Place", "description": "Phenomenology shifts theory from visual composition to embodied experience. Students study body, memory, material, atmosphere, and place as central architectural concerns.", "children": [{"id": "a4bfa92a-626d-4e7e-a872-63639eac46f2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Embodied Perception", "children": [{"id": "818c9c7c-46e8-43b7-926d-45194347a7d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Pallasmaa and the critique of ocularcentrism"}, {"id": "0f31c141-ce2f-4fbb-bb84-cc33d91f859e", "type": "concept", "title": "Touch, sound, smell, movement, and scale"}, {"id": "0431d5fc-23aa-4593-a300-46ce4c2197f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Hapticity, memory, and material presence"}, {"id": "27d6dc1e-4f19-4f4a-94b1-e844b19e6e07", "type": "concept", "title": "The body as measure and participant"}], "description": "Students learn why experience is multisensory, temporal, and bodily."}, {"id": "866be65d-11fa-488b-adc2-1bb08d019e0c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Place, Dwelling, and Meaning", "children": [{"id": "932c0dd6-2042-4dfd-946b-b33ec7b3e327", "type": "concept", "title": "Heidegger, Norberg-Schulz, and dwelling"}, {"id": "41fd88df-7f40-4f77-9de3-e725b475a9d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Genius loci and place-making"}, {"id": "8d6e26e8-771d-4f37-b941-9993d796551b", "type": "concept", "title": "Home, threshold, landscape, and ritual"}, {"id": "c98d72e2-723d-4cc8-b240-1bb7982129a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of nostalgia and essentialism"}], "description": "This subtopic examines architecture as a way of orienting human existence in the world."}, {"id": "4cb0e0e6-e632-4340-babf-1617f3505778", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Atmosphere and Material Poetics", "children": [{"id": "3e81deb2-1332-41ac-90b6-847ca016b754", "type": "concept", "title": "Zumthor and atmospheres"}, {"id": "05f2882b-4571-4a6d-b7fc-1c9606ffa346", "type": "concept", "title": "Light, shadow, temperature, texture, and silence"}, {"id": "5e9988d9-5858-4f93-96f4-f062e74e997b", "type": "concept", "title": "Aging, patina, and weathering"}, {"id": "dc50dd7f-847e-49ab-b9bd-1da16000c933", "type": "concept", "title": "Phenomenology in design critique"}], "description": "Students study how materials, light, detail, weather, and time produce affective environments."}]}, {"id": "034544f9-ade6-4125-8320-8a47a3b96008", "type": "topic", "title": "Semiotics, Meaning, Pop Culture, and Architectural Communication", "description": "This unit treats architecture as sign, language, image, and communication. Students learn how buildings signify through form, ornament, context, media, and use.", "children": [{"id": "a173c203-65b0-4fcf-9997-6ef45612d000", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architecture as Sign System", "children": [{"id": "94778081-ab3f-4e67-9982-4c095091b3dc", "type": "concept", "title": "Signifier, signified, code, and convention"}, {"id": "7c4cb6d8-db90-4d45-8fab-4d40c8bff5d3", "type": "concept", "title": "Icon, index, symbol, and architectural meaning"}, {"id": "69973b4a-e167-4bc6-bf12-992d35adc914", "type": "concept", "title": "Language analogies and their limits"}, {"id": "b6a08c29-a7e0-4bb8-b1fd-ee521cbdf51f", "type": "concept", "title": "Meaning as social and historical"}], "description": "Students examine semiotic approaches that read buildings as communicative systems."}, {"id": "90ba88a0-dffb-4504-b536-fb55de3da9e2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Popular Culture and the Ordinary", "children": [{"id": "4bad2e7b-8803-4037-9cad-78a46b185037", "type": "concept", "title": "Learning from Las Vegas"}, {"id": "6dcdbb67-0b89-4a8e-97ef-a132b3d52849", "type": "concept", "title": "Ducks and decorated sheds"}, {"id": "cc4b143a-6c30-4684-ba17-302e88909469", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial strips, signs, and roadside urbanism"}, {"id": "889d80cc-afb0-4983-841b-3005c8e27be1", "type": "concept", "title": "High culture, low culture, and disciplinary anxiety"}], "description": "Postmodern theory took ordinary landscapes seriously as sources of communication and meaning."}, {"id": "4dca60af-765a-485d-bd4d-e9d1c5c9db82", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and Reception", "children": [{"id": "7031e6ed-c021-48ba-8ec3-ec32b5c0582c", "type": "concept", "title": "Audience, interpretation, and ambiguity"}, {"id": "bf297745-06ce-4344-9f75-e719820f0c1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Branding, image, and spectacle"}, {"id": "69bee12e-6db2-4ba7-b2d0-53327f9a5278", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions and media reception"}, {"id": "f34dbf7d-3b7d-4c5b-8c02-4ef1a36d787b", "type": "concept", "title": "Misreading, appropriation, and popular use"}], "description": "Students learn that meaning is produced through users, critics, photographs, publications, and institutions."}]}, {"id": "4d928244-f58a-46b8-b2cb-b0c31268c78c", "type": "topic", "title": "Postmodernism, Complexity, Contradiction, and Historical Reference", "description": "This section studies postmodernism as a critique of modernist purity and a re-opening of history, symbolism, irony, urban context, and pluralism.", "children": [{"id": "f9429243-998e-4b8d-be84-a9bf9ebee558", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Venturi and Contradiction", "children": [{"id": "a2a4bac2-3f33-44d2-8a98-534c61338b72", "type": "concept", "title": "Both-and rather than either-or"}, {"id": "58f46f7d-12d3-4191-9b0e-ea00623055a3", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, richness, and difficult unity"}, {"id": "c3b55e46-992c-412f-9634-a957d379400d", "type": "concept", "title": "Main Street and ordinary architecture"}, {"id": "5841580a-9ae9-404d-b145-7f3b71c8d8e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Contradiction as design method"}], "description": "Students analyze complexity as a theoretical challenge to reductive modernism."}, {"id": "d5b91e89-ad39-41c5-9de6-6fc4487390f5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "History, Typology, and Memory", "children": [{"id": "cac090d6-94be-4afd-ab3f-a8e7cad79385", "type": "concept", "title": "Rossi, collective memory, and the city"}, {"id": "1d327217-222b-4dc8-b463-3f3950b5c984", "type": "concept", "title": "Typology, monument, and permanence"}, {"id": "1cacf0e9-ace1-4af3-815e-56906d5c8435", "type": "concept", "title": "Contextualism and urban morphology"}, {"id": "2ab2bfd7-b77a-4819-9490-a905a709e82c", "type": "concept", "title": "Pastiche, quotation, and irony"}], "description": "Postmodern theory revived historical reference and urban continuity as theoretical concerns."}, {"id": "a14ec7a5-a1cc-4c8d-b6ab-d08e3a577c2b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Pluralism and Its Critiques", "children": [{"id": "dabd3832-8e95-486f-a3d9-621c7fd781c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Plural meaning and cultural difference"}, {"id": "756d45c5-4f56-4625-9ee0-6f7698196040", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial spectacle and surface"}, {"id": "4e311d48-a683-40a9-906d-9db256ff419c", "type": "concept", "title": "Identity, representation, and inclusion"}, {"id": "d6fb728d-0a52-4a60-b52b-a706df27bd68", "type": "concept", "title": "Postmodernism after postmodernism"}], "description": "Students evaluate postmodernism's liberating and problematic dimensions."}]}, {"id": "0a77e579-4a9c-4c29-8560-792adcd0f279", "type": "topic", "title": "Critical Theory, Marxism, Ideology, and Architectural Production", "description": "This section connects architecture to social power. Students study how buildings and cities reproduce, resist, or reveal capitalism, ideology, class, labor, institutions, and cultural authority.", "children": [{"id": "f2434d0d-b893-4455-becd-529876b9cacb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Marxist and Political-Economic Readings", "children": [{"id": "ad3492aa-ba3f-492c-af5f-bc4e8143c8c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Use value, exchange value, and real estate"}, {"id": "e204e81d-aa4f-457b-9f73-f236afd15bdf", "type": "concept", "title": "Commodification of space"}, {"id": "7fa259d5-6731-4935-a0bf-10ca615a8af0", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, construction, and invisible work"}, {"id": "67ee30a3-0925-4f14-9c0b-7b530c4ac071", "type": "concept", "title": "Housing, rent, and development pressure"}], "description": "Students learn to read architecture through production, labor, land, value, and capital."}, {"id": "42d4a1e4-9420-4327-be30-21a953c93ecd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology, Institutions, and Culture", "children": [{"id": "5dc56293-19a2-4fdb-ba49-ef8c91cbfe04", "type": "concept", "title": "Hegemony and cultural production"}, {"id": "6f5ba4fc-aa75-4426-a688-752173853fd8", "type": "concept", "title": "Museums, schools, awards, and publishing"}, {"id": "e2472e06-34d2-4937-986d-a2c06d51e206", "type": "concept", "title": "Professional norms and disciplinary gatekeeping"}, {"id": "a645f437-c27f-470b-a4a9-d88c81124f4e", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique of neutrality"}], "description": "Architecture participates in making authority appear natural, beautiful, or inevitable."}, {"id": "6cab0bc9-d209-4a3a-945d-409535ef5be2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Critical Practice", "children": [{"id": "999665d3-a3ab-40ef-baac-f0e7140818aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Autonomy versus instrumentality"}, {"id": "9064ba73-8529-496a-b0f3-2d8b85532dee", "type": "concept", "title": "Activism, advocacy, and community design"}, {"id": "f2c0cfa8-cbf2-4d12-acb7-8656e43082eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Institutional critique"}, {"id": "b7912f25-eade-4c00-a2a6-9014f66c25d1", "type": "concept", "title": "Limits of critical formalism"}], "description": "Students ask whether architecture can be politically critical through design, practice, research, or refusal."}]}, {"id": "b2942945-af79-4a26-9ba2-87b9a5d81c13", "type": "topic", "title": "Deconstruction, Disjunction, Event, Diagram, and Late Theory", "description": "This unit studies late twentieth-century theory as architecture engaged philosophy, language, instability, event, and media. Students learn both its conceptual power and its institutional excesses.", "children": [{"id": "8b252071-2b84-4465-a1b6-d45c314e030b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Deconstruction and Architectural Form", "children": [{"id": "1eded5a1-1dbc-498a-a853-3d78e2014729", "type": "concept", "title": "Derrida, Eisenman, Tschumi, Libeskind, and Hadid"}, {"id": "4a526cb8-bcab-4637-b6eb-751252486e2e", "type": "concept", "title": "Text, trace, supplement, and undecidability"}, {"id": "7af0403d-86db-4bd3-b1f0-bf8c12fd462e", "type": "concept", "title": "MoMA Deconstructivist Architecture exhibition"}, {"id": "d9d282e3-d8ab-434e-991b-a61267588fa3", "type": "concept", "title": "Form, philosophy, and spectacle"}], "description": "Students examine fragmentation, trace, absence, and instability in theory and design."}, {"id": "d35a57f4-71ba-4df0-a0ce-460319166c18", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Event, Program, and Disjunction", "children": [{"id": "92499c66-a64d-424a-9b22-5b615b69f315", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture and Disjunction"}, {"id": "aa3fee75-beeb-4770-8ac0-a9ce46da1e3c", "type": "concept", "title": "Event-space, program, movement, and sequence"}, {"id": "4c19085b-b125-4cad-a0f6-d481f5a04fe5", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-programming and montage"}, {"id": "873fdc45-8fe6-4d83-9279-61f240f4d2ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Parc de la Villette as theoretical project"}], "description": "Tschumi and others challenged the idea of architecture as stable form alone."}, {"id": "536912d8-c3bc-4d88-a7d0-0e2f121076e8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Diagram, Index, and Late-Theory Institutions", "children": [{"id": "59d3b4cd-b9c4-4970-a3d9-42cb5e498631", "type": "concept", "title": "Diagram as generator and evidence"}, {"id": "44d41c6d-72fe-40ad-8601-a94e5272e063", "type": "concept", "title": "Journals, schools, exhibitions, and conferences"}, {"id": "3345849c-487b-4b88-bdba-e5ec2082571c", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory culture and studio pedagogy"}, {"id": "e6c39ffc-1ac8-4d68-bb55-9fbb52f9a036", "type": "concept", "title": "Critiques of opacity and jargon"}], "description": "Students learn how diagrams and academic networks shaped late twentieth-century discourse."}]}, {"id": "dd087251-8627-4601-966c-d6c14b38e2b8", "type": "topic", "title": "Gender, Race, Labor, Authorship, and the Limits of the Canon", "description": "This section challenges the architectural canon by asking who gets to theorize, design, build, inhabit, and be remembered. Students connect theory to inequity in authorship, representation, labor, and space.", "children": [{"id": "04a4bb9e-4a98-4097-9694-872c727aaa05", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Feminist and Queer Architectural Theory", "children": [{"id": "800206a2-7ede-430d-b388-7940448805e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Domesticity, care, and reproductive labor"}, {"id": "2b76244c-935c-4181-9e28-f765b546e006", "type": "concept", "title": "Feminist critiques of authorship and studio culture"}, {"id": "7ab8be5b-d08e-4fc1-9f28-15d4471ab573", "type": "concept", "title": "Queer space and non-normative publics"}, {"id": "b5e44132-17df-45ab-bc09-30d02504095b", "type": "concept", "title": "Bodies, bathrooms, safety, and access"}], "description": "Students examine how gender and sexuality reshape spatial analysis and professional history."}, {"id": "270f9370-9cb0-49c6-a996-7f4272447f31", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Race, Modernity, and Spatial Power", "children": [{"id": "c038dbb9-59a9-4680-a87a-9c0fb7e0a1ba", "type": "concept", "title": "Race and Modern Architecture"}, {"id": "d534a994-61e9-4561-8201-b3ebdf3283c3", "type": "concept", "title": "Redlining, urban renewal, and spatial segregation"}, {"id": "d4cb8359-3eb9-46fc-a900-3702ae6bb2ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Black, Indigenous, and minority architects and theorists"}, {"id": "754b47b3-1aa7-46af-9521-15d423a3978b", "type": "concept", "title": "Monuments, memory, and spatial justice"}], "description": "This subtopic connects architecture to racial capitalism, coloniality, segregation, and representation."}, {"id": "d43f17ce-8a17-4b50-a9e6-588ff63cd4f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Labor, Credit, and Collective Production", "children": [{"id": "9c8de9fd-5719-46ea-a5d3-55213bd56baf", "type": "concept", "title": "Office labor and invisible collaborators"}, {"id": "77f815cf-5ba9-4c61-9658-efd848aaca43", "type": "concept", "title": "Construction labor and material supply chains"}, {"id": "b17273a4-18eb-477b-b839-2ae44e714570", "type": "concept", "title": "Credit, archives, and intellectual property"}, {"id": "aa1b612c-1874-4238-b1aa-068f66c4bda4", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorship beyond the master architect"}], "description": "Students learn that architecture is produced collectively, even when theory celebrates individual authors."}]}, {"id": "4c058d43-7f07-47b6-bfa7-416ca47376a8", "type": "topic", "title": "Globalization, Colonialism, Decolonial Critique, and Comparative Theory", "description": "This unit expands architectural theory beyond Euro-American universals. Students learn to read theory through translation, empire, migration, climate, development, and situated knowledge.", "children": [{"id": "5c273502-2c88-4f2e-b02e-aaef4ab9df01", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Colonial and Imperial Architectural Knowledge", "children": [{"id": "e2f7eb56-8a98-4e77-acd8-77e1d3fb2ac3", "type": "concept", "title": "Colonial planning and administrative architecture"}, {"id": "edc12635-891a-4e37-8c92-8ce5cda5b2aa", "type": "concept", "title": "Missionary, educational, and extractive spaces"}, {"id": "c82e9315-582c-4de8-a813-022ea22dd203", "type": "concept", "title": "Tropical modernism and climatic expertise"}, {"id": "0d7c94f9-b6ec-4012-ace8-6041b6c16c1b", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, surveys, and knowledge production"}], "description": "Students examine how architectural theory traveled through unequal power relations."}, {"id": "24f726ec-2f7d-48a8-82a2-87699e9f9b1c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Global Modernisms and Translation", "children": [{"id": "e55843bc-263e-4c94-9f97-abe1eff57ace", "type": "concept", "title": "Latin American, African, South Asian, East Asian, and Middle Eastern modernisms"}, {"id": "1cf05cd3-5b53-4b13-b8b8-c520a1318270", "type": "concept", "title": "Nation-building and postcolonial institutions"}, {"id": "9a61c33e-0ff5-460a-9b59-1cdc65e9fa0c", "type": "concept", "title": "Local materials, climate, and labor"}, {"id": "063509ef-4bf5-4875-a8f5-4acfd2c255a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Translation, hybridity, and misrecognition"}], "description": "This subtopic studies modern architecture as multiple, situated, and contested rather than simply exported."}, {"id": "7efeba50-ba1c-4e31-8cb6-41ecbbad48be", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Decolonial and Comparative Theory", "children": [{"id": "2027e513-613e-48da-b617-d2b5b16311d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Decolonial critique and epistemic justice"}, {"id": "66dac322-4d01-422b-adb8-04fd731ef420", "type": "concept", "title": "Indigenous spatial knowledge and sovereignty"}, {"id": "9d2d3780-9dde-4684-aacd-968680269112", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparative method and its risks"}, {"id": "a9c5f28a-5c6f-46ec-a4b7-9a7e48cfed75", "type": "concept", "title": "Against tokenism and essentialism"}], "description": "Students develop methods for theory that do not treat the West as default center."}]}, {"id": "d22ae319-9505-4bc9-86a6-7d44b2c72d07", "type": "topic", "title": "Urban Theory, Density, Everyday Life, Housing, and Public Space", "description": "This section connects architectural theory to urban life. Students study streets, housing, infrastructure, public space, density, informality, and everyday use as theoretical problems.", "children": [{"id": "62a31b2d-a90c-46b5-a097-2103bee38036", "type": "subtopic", "title": "The City as Theoretical Object", "children": [{"id": "67dc889a-d87a-4b2e-b190-3b950e0dea59", "type": "concept", "title": "Simmel, Benjamin, Lefebvre, Jacobs, Koolhaas, and others"}, {"id": "af9d5ce6-6633-4031-8621-d3dbc9b0be46", "type": "concept", "title": "Public space, crowds, and everyday life"}, {"id": "39432ef0-128a-4402-b914-916533520d72", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure and metropolitan systems"}, {"id": "53163425-9e97-4988-9d47-6ae42ac9006c", "type": "concept", "title": "The city as text, machine, and ecosystem"}], "description": "Students learn to theorize the city through morphology, infrastructure, economy, governance, and experience."}, {"id": "1c73edad-6e96-4062-b0d0-e6ded712ef9f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Housing and Social Reproduction", "children": [{"id": "05f060bd-34e6-41af-9478-1246251a5de2", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum dwelling and mass housing"}, {"id": "f74a6a9d-4cc6-4993-9443-bd543a0a407f", "type": "concept", "title": "Domestic labor, care, and family forms"}, {"id": "b1caee41-cc9f-413c-ba60-e7734a3e50b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Affordability, displacement, and homelessness"}, {"id": "a7f0e866-5e1f-4c2d-8f07-b62df82ea9b4", "type": "concept", "title": "Cooperative, informal, and community-led housing"}], "description": "Architecture theory becomes concrete in the politics of housing."}, {"id": "edec3e31-c033-45d8-817a-435375687168", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Publics, Protest, and Spatial Justice", "children": [{"id": "1fbd2454-f36b-453b-9aba-7fa288bdc116", "type": "concept", "title": "Squares, streets, parks, campuses, and civic interiors"}, {"id": "81c7609c-cd9b-4795-99fd-2f18abd7c3e7", "type": "concept", "title": "Protest, policing, surveillance, and assembly"}, {"id": "e9bc6e08-91de-444d-858e-27958ae6ca14", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility, safety, and exclusion"}, {"id": "4bf3295e-5ed4-4ca0-b54d-300f601b32ed", "type": "concept", "title": "Informality, repair, and commoning"}], "description": "Students study public space as contested political terrain."}]}, {"id": "866f0c5e-9053-4fc1-a776-64fa902d6b77", "type": "topic", "title": "Media, Representation, Visual Culture, and Digital Theory", "description": "This unit studies the media through which architecture is imagined, circulated, judged, and monetized. Students learn that representation does not merely depict architecture; it shapes architectural reality.", "children": [{"id": "39dc64f3-9eb1-42f1-962d-bb32ec142fbe", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Drawing, Photography, and Exhibition", "children": [{"id": "6b24c5f7-13d8-4c1a-9ccb-521bfe9b0542", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan, section, axonometric, collage, and diagram"}, {"id": "5c935d9f-b719-41a4-9fe7-e996fc66af60", "type": "concept", "title": "Photography and the modern canon"}, {"id": "2554ba52-78bc-411e-9af1-aedd3c8560f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Exhibitions, biennales, and curatorial argument"}, {"id": "76abf08b-0cb5-4a7b-82b4-8acc972d5f6e", "type": "concept", "title": "Archives, collections, and institutional framing"}], "description": "Students examine representational media as theoretical instruments."}, {"id": "a09f4c6b-0f05-4be0-a871-b13658fd1348", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Visual Culture and Spectacle", "children": [{"id": "6042a2a3-6488-4e65-b973-2375af4a1ef4", "type": "concept", "title": "Iconicity and city branding"}, {"id": "0081451c-6739-46d8-86a3-fe105c148447", "type": "concept", "title": "Starchitects and global image economies"}, {"id": "91fe3d96-b151-40b4-b4a9-90dcc6e2b0f5", "type": "concept", "title": "Magazines, websites, renders, and social media"}, {"id": "fdbcc800-51e2-41d1-b099-bbe0df7b050b", "type": "concept", "title": "Spectacle versus lived experience"}], "description": "Architecture operates in a culture of images, attention, branding, and tourism."}, {"id": "b1809ec9-bcd0-414d-97f6-c14544e8c942", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Digital Theory and Computational Culture", "children": [{"id": "9d99dc21-2e9d-4a2a-81ac-6e67f5727b45", "type": "concept", "title": "Parametricism and algorithmic design"}, {"id": "25746684-e37b-4fb8-9b49-bd43d9a7985b", "type": "concept", "title": "BIM, platforms, automation, and data"}, {"id": "b1533151-a6bb-4588-ad6f-6e3f40ef21f3", "type": "concept", "title": "Digital fabrication and material intelligence"}, {"id": "1225dfbe-640b-45dc-a535-95171c91b56c", "type": "concept", "title": "AI, authorship, and design judgment"}], "description": "Students assess how computation changes form, authorship, labor, and publics."}]}, {"id": "ac72e1d9-dedd-4624-8e3c-7dfe16355e2b", "type": "topic", "title": "Ecology, Technology, Materiality, and Posthuman Debates", "description": "This section addresses theory under climate crisis. Students examine architecture as part of energy systems, material flows, nonhuman agencies, technologies, and planetary responsibilities.", "children": [{"id": "3a06f480-68a9-44d1-a059-7da4b53a32eb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ecological Thought and Climate Theory", "children": [{"id": "a5b85fc4-f309-45a4-9491-91f058ec3e1e", "type": "concept", "title": "Sustainability, resilience, adaptation, and mitigation"}, {"id": "dbdfa065-47b8-442d-a2e5-4f54d1be42c2", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy, carbon, and operational systems"}, {"id": "091661e2-6a8d-414d-9e56-441ce49ae95f", "type": "concept", "title": "Environmental justice and uneven vulnerability"}, {"id": "6f09a069-2eaa-4a7e-9c11-9c8b65471d47", "type": "concept", "title": "Architecture after growth and extraction"}], "description": "Students learn how climate changes the object and ethics of architecture."}, {"id": "7bab5adb-54ff-409f-ae33-5c7fdd98762a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Materiality and Agency", "children": [{"id": "2ef763dc-b3cb-4acc-b9d2-d66523ee4631", "type": "concept", "title": "Matter, weathering, maintenance, and decay"}, {"id": "b49d1b19-4ff2-4a3c-90fb-d3eaee3716af", "type": "concept", "title": "New materialism and material agency"}, {"id": "03c833f4-ebdc-44fb-97a9-d2234bccc498", "type": "concept", "title": "Toxicity, waste, and embodied carbon"}, {"id": "0ea94a6d-ab48-4b5d-9b81-f7c47f8af0f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Reuse, repair, and circularity"}], "description": "This subtopic treats materials as active participants in architectural life."}, {"id": "9cd606a8-94b9-4e30-8583-e072a92f76ab", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technology, Infrastructure, and Posthuman Questions", "children": [{"id": "a51c8080-be1d-4b13-9542-4229e5c2542b", "type": "concept", "title": "Infrastructure, logistics, and planetary urbanization"}, {"id": "669ec03c-439c-45cc-b28b-7128bd0d37fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Smart buildings, sensors, and surveillance"}, {"id": "9749f911-8714-43d9-abdc-1ec10d61279d", "type": "concept", "title": "Animals, plants, microbes, and multispecies design"}, {"id": "7150b4a5-99ea-4cd4-8ca2-13173fe21c3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Posthuman ethics and design limits"}], "description": "Students ask how architectural theory changes when humans are not the only actors."}]}, {"id": "ca034601-2434-4142-a57b-a9012bcacc1f", "type": "topic", "title": "Contemporary Debates: Practice, Ethics, Crisis, Preservation, and Futures", "description": "This unit gathers live debates so students can position themselves critically. The aim is to connect theory to professional, public, and design decisions under real constraints.", "children": [{"id": "cbad1846-578e-4e68-b997-de0cf54ab513", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Practice and Ethics", "children": [{"id": "0ac4fe02-64bb-4116-8520-d608ca60dc2a", "type": "concept", "title": "Client, public, and planetary responsibilities"}, {"id": "a0e66a35-ebd4-42a4-a53d-2e623e671a77", "type": "concept", "title": "Labor, procurement, and fee structures"}, {"id": "8e13fcc8-c90a-4183-8437-34607fa1e314", "type": "concept", "title": "Participation, expertise, and accountability"}, {"id": "0e6097fb-c4fe-4df8-8a1b-93fb7663965d", "type": "concept", "title": "Refusal, advocacy, and alternative practice models"}], "description": "Students examine how theoretical commitments become professional choices."}, {"id": "002b1646-9d6d-4716-a7bd-4bbd845f806c", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Crisis, Preservation, and Adaptation", "children": [{"id": "0875f6d8-6ea4-4eec-9aab-fe7459f62821", "type": "concept", "title": "Adaptive reuse and retrofit"}, {"id": "a011a1bb-5abb-4243-8c95-82128c5dd207", "type": "concept", "title": "Preservation, memory, and contested heritage"}, {"id": "65509aef-b2e2-483e-8aac-9e3f95caa892", "type": "concept", "title": "Disaster, migration, and temporary shelter"}, {"id": "2b5216e6-68d8-46e1-92e0-40b3329b5eb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Repair as design theory"}], "description": "Contemporary theory must address existing buildings, damaged worlds, and limited resources."}, {"id": "ab86d9a3-bebe-44ca-888c-3e352d2744d4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Futures of the Discipline", "children": [{"id": "00819711-3100-41a6-9784-8b74a6c6ee2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate-constrained design culture"}, {"id": "35a18db9-9205-4a41-a8c2-19a786aa44c8", "type": "concept", "title": "AI, automation, and authorship"}, {"id": "d5452f8c-52db-4abd-85a3-30907b13fe82", "type": "concept", "title": "Post-professional and interdisciplinary practice"}, {"id": "ad9f8691-8ec1-4b9f-977b-b78dcecb51fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Theory as future-making and critique"}], "description": "Students speculate responsibly about what architecture can become."}]}, {"id": "ad078920-93a8-4a53-8fdf-e7da8b3e9ee6", "type": "topic", "title": "Theory Research Lab: Close Reading, Argument, and Design Translation", "description": "The capstone section turns theory into scholarly and design work. Students learn to read closely, argue clearly, facilitate discussion, and translate theory into a design position without flattening it into slogans.", "children": [{"id": "eff6639d-cc3b-441e-81cf-dbc1bf78fef6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Close Reading and Concept Mapping", "children": [{"id": "145ec4ff-ade4-4f69-a043-13f331b2003e", "type": "concept", "title": "Thesis, method, evidence, and audience"}, {"id": "5f71a626-4291-4b65-b97f-6c31817c9dbe", "type": "concept", "title": "Key terms and conceptual genealogies"}, {"id": "01daf74b-d39d-4898-9912-c7f6b59f0473", "type": "concept", "title": "Productive confusion and seminar questions"}, {"id": "7bd8af55-d04e-4cb6-91a9-de6df3c938ab", "type": "concept", "title": "Comparing texts without false equivalence"}], "description": "Students practice reading difficult texts by extracting terms, claims, evidence, stakes, and contradictions."}, {"id": "294005af-e69a-41f8-9408-73cc278a7ea0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Research Methods and Writing", "children": [{"id": "5008093a-f10d-469e-9553-0e1f5c72b94d", "type": "concept", "title": "Research question and historiographic context"}, {"id": "f1dff4fa-92e9-4ba0-8eaf-18461123ca53", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary texts, archives, visual evidence, and secondary scholarship"}, {"id": "3b50f299-6102-4b58-a777-ba33b813af61", "type": "concept", "title": "Abstracts, annotated bibliography, and outline"}, {"id": "634943aa-cdf5-4287-bb91-ca3738330d7a", "type": "concept", "title": "Drafting, peer review, revision, and citation"}], "description": "This subtopic builds a paper from sources, buildings, images, and arguments."}, {"id": "0c12376f-ef7d-4a1e-9040-017f1ff1df59", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Design-Theory Translation", "children": [{"id": "2760a2ce-faf1-4956-b225-e69a3271bed1", "type": "concept", "title": "Design position statement"}, {"id": "bdee7370-7bd3-48b5-ad4c-9658506dfe97", "type": "concept", "title": "Precedent analysis through theory"}, {"id": "2d480056-aab5-4535-bc58-4a4823105789", "type": "concept", "title": "Critique language and studio application"}, {"id": "d59c7102-0d08-447e-8c2d-36623e19a80d", "type": "concept", "title": "Final seminar presentation and reflection"}], "description": "Students learn to connect theory to design practice with rigor and restraint."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$tharjson$::jsonb,
